# GNU wc and the spec disagree on what a word is and on invalid bytes:
# - POSIX: a word is a maximal string of characters that are not white space
#   (the space class of LC_CTYPE). GNU only counts a word that holds a printable
#   character, so a run of NUL, of bytes >= 0x80 in the C locale, of U+FFFE or
#   U+2028 (iswprint() is false for them) is no word and U+2028/2029 do not
#   separate words. We follow the spec. This shows in -w and in the default output.
# - -m on bytes that are no character in UTF-8: undefined by the spec; GNU leaves
#   them out of the count, we count each one as a character of its own.
# Every other count (lines, bytes, and characters of valid text, emoji, ZWJ and
# tag sequences, flags, keycaps, variation selectors) is the same as GNU.
-w utf8
-mw utf8
-w bad
nulhigh
-m nulhigh
-w nulhigh
-lwm nulhigh
e5
-w e5
-lwm e5
badtail
-m badtail
-w badtail
-lwm badtail
pair2
-w pair2
-lwm pair2
emoji.basic emoji.zwj emoji.flags
-w emoji.basic emoji.zwj emoji.flags cjk.lines
-lwm emoji.sentence mixed.scripts cjk.lines combining
basic noeol ws7 pair1 pair2 emoji.noeol
